Sure. You could use the #include directive in C++ to import libraries and use C functions. The general syntax is as follows: `#include directive-name`. The directive name represents the library you want to include. The library will contain the function signatures that you can use in your code. For example, to include the std namespace in C++, you would use the directive `#include <iostream>`.
Here is an example of using the std namespace:
```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello World!\n";
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, `std::cout` is a function in the `<iostream>` library that you can use to print text to the screen.

This is just a basic introduction to using C++ libraries. There are many other libraries and features available in C++ that you can use to build complex and powerful programs.

